      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Kenedy proudly calls itself the Horned Lizard Capital of Texas, celebrating the state’s iconic reptile.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kenedychamber.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In April 1942, the Kenedy Alien Detention Camp opened, housing 456 Germans, 156 Japanese, and 14 Italians deported from Latin America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenedy%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1944, the camp transitioned to a German POW facility, later also holding Japanese prisoners until the war’s end.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenedy%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Just two miles south of Kenedy lies the Connally Unit, a state prison that gained notoriety in 2000 when the Texas Seven escaped.",
